1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,423
Sally:

2
00:00:00,423 --> 00:00:01,147
Good morning.

3
00:00:01,147 --> 00:00:03,577
Hinchingbrooke Country Park, Sally speaking.

4
00:00:03,577 --> 00:00:06,260
I’m one of the rangers.

5
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:06,601
John:

6
00:00:06,601 --> 00:00:07,283
Oh hello.

7
00:00:07,283 --> 00:00:08,989
My name’s John Chapman,

8
00:00:08,989 --> 00:00:14,789
and I’m a teaching assistant at a local primary school.I’ve been asked to arrange

9
00:00:14,789 --> 00:00:18,200
a visit to the park for two of our classes.

10
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:18,584
Sally:

11
00:00:18,584 --> 00:00:18,968
OK.

12
00:00:18,968 --> 00:00:21,270
What would you like to know?

13
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:21,634
John:

14
00:00:21,634 --> 00:00:24,186
Well, I’m new to this area,

15
00:00:24,186 --> 00:00:28,560
so perhaps you could tell me something about the park first, please.

16
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:29,245
Sally:

17
00:00:29,245 --> 00:00:30,614
Of course.

18
00:00:30,614 --> 00:00:34,722
Altogether the park covers 170 acres,

19
00:00:34,722 --> 00:00:37,460
that’s 69 hectares.

20
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:37,973
Sally:

21
00:00:37,973 --> 00:00:41,567
There are three main types of habitat:

22
00:00:41,567 --> 00:00:43,620
wetland, grassland and woodland.

23
00:00:43,620 --> 00:00:44,032
Sally:

24
00:00:44,032 --> 00:00:48,567
The woods are well established and varied, with an oak plantation,

25
00:00:48,567 --> 00:00:51,040
and other areas of mixed species.

26
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:51,555
John:

27
00:00:51,555 --> 00:00:52,070
Right.

28
00:00:52,070 --> 00:00:52,522
Sally:

29
00:00:52,522 --> 00:00:55,238
The wetland is quite varied, too.

30
00:00:55,238 --> 00:01:01,120
The original farmland was dug up around 40 years ago to extract gravel.

31
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:01,551
Sally:

32
00:01:01,551 --> 00:01:07,579
Once this work was completed, the gravel pits filled with water, forming the two

33
00:01:07,579 --> 00:01:08,440
large lakes.

34
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:08,869
Sally:

35
00:01:08,869 --> 00:01:14,871
There are also several smaller ones, ponds and a stream that flows through the

36
00:01:14,871 --> 00:01:15,300
park.

37
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:15,759
John:

38
00:01:15,759 --> 00:01:16,218
OK,

39
00:01:16,218 --> 00:01:22,640
so I suppose with these different habitats there’s quite a variety of wildlife.

40
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:23,118
Sally:

41
00:01:23,118 --> 00:01:28,854
There certainly is – a lot of different species of birds and insects,

42
00:01:28,854 --> 00:01:32,200
and also animals like deer and rabbits.

43
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:32,662
John:

44
00:01:32,662 --> 00:01:37,280
And I understand you organise educational visits for school parties.

45
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:37,679
Sally:

46
00:01:37,679 --> 00:01:38,878
That’s right.

47
00:01:38,878 --> 00:01:44,471
We can organise a wide range of activities and adapt them to suit all

48
00:01:44,471 --> 00:01:44,870
ages.

49
00:01:44,870 --> 00:01:45,215
John:

50
00:01:45,215 --> 00:01:48,320
Can you give me some examples of the activities?

51
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:48,787
Sally:

52
00:01:48,787 --> 00:01:54,389
Well, one focus is on science, where we help children to discover

53
00:01:54,389 --> 00:01:57,190
and study plants, trees and insects.

54
00:01:57,190 --> 00:01:57,584
Sally:

55
00:01:57,584 --> 00:02:01,920
They also collect and analyse data about the things they see.

56
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:02,325
John:

57
00:02:02,325 --> 00:02:02,730
Uhuh.

58
00:02:02,730 --> 00:02:03,154
Sally:

59
00:02:03,154 --> 00:02:05,274
Another focus is on geography.

60
00:02:05,274 --> 00:02:10,785
The park is a great environment to learn and practise reading a map

61
00:02:10,785 --> 00:02:14,600
and using a compass to navigate around the park.

62
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:15,089
John:

63
00:02:15,089 --> 00:02:18,510
Do you do anything connected with history?

64
00:02:18,510 --> 00:02:18,920
Sally:

65
00:02:18,920 --> 00:02:20,152
Yes, we do.

66
00:02:20,152 --> 00:02:25,897
For instance, the children can explore how the use of the land has changed

67
00:02:25,897 --> 00:02:26,718
over time.

68
00:02:26,718 --> 00:02:29,180
Then there’s leisure and tourism.

69
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:29,639
John:

70
00:02:29,639 --> 00:02:33,310
That focuses on your visitors, I would imagine.

71
00:02:33,310 --> 00:02:33,769
Sally:

72
00:02:33,769 --> 00:02:34,686
Yes, mostly.

73
00:02:34,686 --> 00:02:40,647
The children find out about them, their requirements, the problems they may cause

74
00:02:40,647 --> 00:02:42,940
and how we manage these.

75
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:43,451
Sally:

76
00:02:43,451 --> 00:02:47,030
And another subject we cover is music:

77
00:02:47,030 --> 00:02:53,677
here the children experiment with natural materials to create sounds and explore rhythm

78
00:02:53,677 --> 00:02:54,700
and tempo.

79
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:54,998
John:

80
00:02:54,998 --> 00:02:56,190
That must be fun!

81
00:02:56,190 --> 00:02:57,210
Sally:

82
00:02:57,210 --> 00:03:02,310
Most children really enjoy it.

83
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:02,830
Sally:

84
00:03:02,830 --> 00:03:07,510
And of course, all the activities are educational, too.

85
00:03:07,510 --> 00:03:12,190
Learning outside the classroom encourages children to be creative,

86
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:15,830
and to explore and discover for themselves.

87
00:03:15,830 --> 00:03:16,146
John:

88
00:03:16,146 --> 00:03:20,562
I would imagine they get a sense of freedom that might not be a

89
00:03:20,562 --> 00:03:22,140
normal part of their lives.

90
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:22,539
Sally:

91
00:03:22,539 --> 00:03:23,736
That’s right.

92
00:03:23,736 --> 00:03:29,722
And very often the children discover that they can do things they didn’t know

93
00:03:29,722 --> 00:03:30,919
they could do,

94
00:03:30,919 --> 00:03:32,915
and they develop new skills.

95
00:03:32,915 --> 00:03:34,910
This gives them greater self-confidence.

96
00:03:34,910 --> 00:03:35,380
John:

97
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:36,789
It sounds great.

98
00:03:36,789 --> 00:03:40,546
So, what about the practical side of it?

99
00:03:40,546 --> 00:03:44,773
How much does it cost for a full-day visit?

100
00:03:44,773 --> 00:03:49,470
We would expect to bring between 30 and 40 children.

101
00:03:49,470 --> 00:03:49,888
Sally:

102
00:03:49,888 --> 00:03:56,152
If there are over 30, it costs £4.95 for each child who attends on

103
00:03:56,152 --> 00:03:56,987
the day.

104
00:03:56,987 --> 00:03:58,658
We invoice you afterwards,

105
00:03:58,658 --> 00:04:03,252
so you don’t pay for children who can’t come

106
00:04:03,252 --> 00:04:05,340
because of sickness, for example.

107
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:05,694
Sally:

108
00:04:05,694 --> 00:04:10,643
There’s no charge for leaders and other adults – as many as you want

109
00:04:10,643 --> 00:04:11,350
to bring.

110
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:11,700
John:

111
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,098
That sounds very fair.

112
00:04:13,098 --> 00:04:15,197
Well, thanks for all the information.

113
00:04:15,197 --> 00:04:18,344
I’ll need to discuss it with my colleagues,

114
00:04:18,344 --> 00:04:22,890
and I hope to get back to you soon to make a booking.

115
00:04:22,890 --> 00:04:23,242
Sally:

116
00:04:23,242 --> 00:04:26,058
We’ll look forward to hearing from you.

117
00:04:26,058 --> 00:04:26,410
Goodbye.

118
00:04:26,410 --> 00:04:27,168
John:

119
00:04:27,168 --> 00:04:27,926
Goodbye,

120
00:04:27,926 --> 00:04:30,200
and thank you.
